{
  "dom_id": "step-q35117",
  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "Find the interval where the function $f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable, given that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =|t-1|\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{1-t}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & ={t}^{2}\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9}\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $|t-1|$ is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=1.$\n\n- The second component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $\\sqrt{1-t}$ is differentiable on $(-\\infty ,1)$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=1$ and the function is not defined for $t>1.$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,1).$ \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component ${t}^{2}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The second component $\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9}$ is differentiable for $(-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t)$ is differentiable only on $(-\\infty ,3).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,-3).$ \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for both functions is $(-\\infty ,1)\\cap (-\\infty ,-3)=(-\\infty ,-3).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
